  "title": "The Prime Number Race",
  "statement": {
    "representation": "markdown-latex",
    "text": "Let $\\pi(n; a, b)$ be the number of primes $p \\le n$ with $p \\equiv a \\pmod b$. For every $a$ and $b$ with $a \\perp b$, are there infinitely many values of $n$ for which $\\pi(n; a, b) > \\pi(n; a_1, b)$ for every $a_1 \\not\\equiv a \\pmod b$?",
